#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 5
#: rc.cpp:1522
#, no-c-format
msgid "Watchglas"
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 6
#: rc.cpp:1525
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Watchglasses are round glass panes with a diameter of circa 5 - 10 cm which are "
"used in the laboratory technics. Their border is a bit bent up so that you can "
"fill them with little amounts of liquids in order to let them evaporate. The "
"evaporation can be accelerated by heating the watchglass with a Bunsen burner. "
"Watchglassen also can be used to cover petri dishes or beakers. Moreover "
"watchglasses are suited to weigh little amounts of matter or alternatively dry "
"them in the airing cupboard. The term watchglass is derived from the former "
"pocket watches' protection glass which was often domed."
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 10
#: rc.cpp:1528
#, no-c-format
msgid "Dehydrator"
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 13
#: rc.cpp:1531
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A dehydrator is a laboratory apparatus which is often made of glass and serves "
"to dry chemical matters, means for deprivation of water alternatively liquid.\n"
"\t\t\tIn principle it is a glass bowl with cap which has a planar grinding so "
"that the bowl is air tight closeable. The dehydrator's lower part is ordinarily "
"filled with a drying agent e.g. calcium chloride, silica gel, phosphoric "
"anhydride or sulphuric acid. On a diaphanous cartridge of plastic or ceramics "
"above the drying agent the matter is put in order to dry.\n"
"\t\t\tThe drying agent is hygroscopic that means it dehumidifies its "
"environment and absorbs the humidity. The atmosphere in the dehydrator dryed by "
"the drying agent dehumidifies water alternatively dissolver tself from the "
"matter to dry."
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 17
#: rc.cpp:1536
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
msgid "Spattle"
msgstr "Water"

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 18
#: rc.cpp:1539
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
" The spattle is a laboratory apparatus to scrape off, hackle and transport "
"chemicals. The material they are made of (e.g. iron, titan, platinum) and their "
"design (e.g. flat spattle or spoon spattle) can be different."
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 22
#: rc.cpp:1542
#, no-c-format
msgid "Water Jet Pump"
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 23
#: rc.cpp:1545
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The water jet pump has two entries and one exit and consists in principle of "
"two pipes that are sticked into each other. At the water entry a water jet "
"shoots under full fuel-line pressure out of a nozzle into a bit larger pipe. "
"Thereby the water jet carries away air or fluid from the second entry. "
"<br> this is caused by the negativ pressure in a drifting fluid. Thus it is an "
"appliance of the hydrodynamic paradox. This says that objects close to drifting "
"fluids are aspirated instead of being pushed away."
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 27
#: rc.cpp:1548
#, no-c-format
msgid "Refractometer"
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 28
#: rc.cpp:1551
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With a refractometer the refraction index of optical medias is determined. If "
"the refraction index of a chemical is known it can be used after a synthesis to "
"determine the product's pureness or to audit the synthesis' success."
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 32
#: rc.cpp:1554
#, no-c-format
msgid "Mortar"
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 33
#: rc.cpp:1557
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In a mortar solid matters are mechanically hackled. Also you can homogenise a "
"mixture of e.g. salts through grating using a mortar. The thruster for "
"levigating is called pestle."
msgstr ""

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 37
#: rc.cpp:1560
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
msgid "Heating Coil"
msgstr "Smelt punt:"

#. i18n: file data/tools.xml line 38
#: rc.cpp:1563
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Heating coils are used to heat flasks and other containers. Multiple heating "
"coils can be connected with a thermometer so that the heat will not exceed a "
"specified temperature. It is possible to use a magnetic field inside the "
"heating coil to stir fluids with a magnetic stirrer. This will homogenize the "
"fluid in terms of temperature and composition."
msgstr ""

